1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
What time do you start work?
你什么时候开始工作呢？

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:09,690
Do you prefer a leisurely start to the day, or are you an early bird who likes to get going at the crack of dawn?
你是喜欢悠闲地开始新的一天，还是说你是个早起的人，喜欢在破晓时分就开始忙碌呢？

3
00:00:09,690 --> 00:00:15,140
At one computer programming company in the US you have no such choice.
在美国的一家计算机编程公司，你没有这样的选择。

4
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:22,800
Work begins at the same oddly specific time every day: 9.06am.
每天的工作都在一个奇怪的特定时间开始：上午9点06分。

5
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:35,940
In contrast to a more conventional 9am kick-off, employees in Pivotal Software's 20 offices around the globe have to be at work and ready to go precisely six minutes later.
与更传统的上午9点开始工作不同，Pivotal Software公司在全球的20个办公室的员工必须在9点整过6分钟后，准时到岗并做好工作准备。

6
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:43,000
At that time a cowbell is rung and employees gather for a brief stand-up meeting.
在那个时候，会敲响牛铃，员工们聚在一起开一个简短的站立会议。

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,860
After that it's nose to the grindstone, with no other meetings or distractions throughout the working day.
之后大家就开始埋头苦干，在整个工作日里没有其他会议或者干扰。

8
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:56,120
They steam on through until 6pm sharp, at which point everybody downs tools.
他们一直忙碌到下午6点整，到那个时候，所有人都停止工作。

9
00:00:56,120 --> 00:01:01,340
So what's the reason behind this quirky timekeeping?
那么，这种古怪的计时方式背后的原因是什么呢？

10
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:10,080
Company CEO Rob Mee explains it's about making his team of computer programmers as efficient as possible.
公司首席执行官罗布·米解释说，这是为了让他的计算机程序员团队尽可能高效。

11
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:17,950
"I realised that programmers, if left to their own devices, may roll in at 10am," he says.
“我意识到，如果让程序员们自行其是，他们可能会在上午10点才到公司。”

12
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:27,470
"We thought that if we made it 9am, developers psyching themselves up for the day would think, 'well if it is 9am I'll be late.'"
他说，“我们认为，如果规定9点上班，那些为新一天做准备的开发人员会想，‘嗯，如果已经9点了，那我就迟到了。’”

13
00:01:27,470 --> 00:01:41,540
He needed a little more leeway, but a starting time of 9.05am on the nose felt too rigidly precise — whereas 9.06am seemed “fun”.
他需要多一点回旋余地，但正正好好9点05分这个时间点感觉太严格地精确了——而9点06分似乎“更有趣”。

14
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:45,260
Mr Mee was also thinking of their stomachs.
米先生也考虑到了员工的胃。

15
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:50,690
Lazier programmers who turned up around 10am would also often have missed breakfast.
那些比较懒、大约上午10点出现在公司的程序员常常也会错过早餐。

16
00:01:50,690 --> 00:01:59,790
This meant they would be hungry by 11am and take an early lunch, leaving them with an interminably long working afternoon ahead.
这就意味着他们在上午11点就会饿，然后提前吃午饭，这样他们接下来的下午工作时间就会无限地漫长。

17
00:01:59,790 --> 00:02:06,880
So he decided to provide breakfast as an extra incentive to get in to the office on time.
所以他决定提供早餐，作为让员工准时到达办公室的额外激励措施。

18
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:17,910
The company's approach to punctuality may seem nerdy, but Pivotal Software is in fact a highly successful company, valued at 20bn RMB.
该公司对守时的做法可能看起来有点书呆子气，但Pivotal Software实际上是一家非常成功的公司，估值200亿元人民币。

19
00:02:17,910 --> 00:02:25,330
And Pivotal is not alone: Silicon Valley has long been associated with an alternative working culture.
而且Pivotal并非个例：硅谷长期以来一直与一种非传统的工作文化联系在一起。

20
00:02:25,330 --> 00:02:32,940
Google has 'nap pods' and meditation classes, while Netflix offers a year's paid parental leave.
谷歌有“午睡舱”和冥想课程，而网飞公司提供一年的带薪育儿假。
